  "title": "The primitive rays generate the cone.",
  "body": "The primitive rays generate the cone A strongly convex rational polyhedral cone is generated by its finitely many primitive ray generators. Its zero cone has the empty generating list.  For a nonzero cone, discard zero vectors from an integral generating list and choose strictly positive on them. Its section by is exactly Indeed normalizing a nonnegative conical combination gives a convex combination of these normalized vectors, and the converse is immediate. A finite convex hull is the convex hull of its vertices: remove a listed point whenever it is a convex combination of the others, until no such point remains.  Each remaining vertex is strictly separated from the convex hull of the other remaining points. Subtracting its value times from the separating linear functional makes the functional zero at that vertex and positive at every other vertex. It is consequently nonnegative on the whole cone, and its zero face is precisely the ray through the chosen vertex. When the section has only one vertex, the original cone is itself that ray. Thus the cone is generated by these rays. Conversely, an exposed one-dimensional face can contain only one of the section vertices and must contain one, by the face property for a generating sum. These are all its rays. Their rational directions have primitive lattice generators; replacing a nonzero rational generator by its primitive one leaves the cone unchanged.  "
