  "body": " A Gorenstein cone without a crepant toric resolution  For , let Its only lattice points are its vertices. Indeed, at integral height a point has coefficient at the last vertex. Its integral coordinates must be at least one, while the barycentric sum condition says , impossible for . The height-zero base is a unimodular triangle, and height contains only the top vertex.  The cone over in is Gorenstein, with height the last coordinate, and terminal by the criterion. Its four ray vectors have determinant of absolute value , so it is singular. A crepant toric resolution could insert only height-one rays, but no further such lattice point exists. A simplicial cone cannot be subdivided using only its original rays. Thus no crepant toric resolution exists. This is a statement about toric resolutions; no assertion about arbitrary nontoric resolutions is needed.  "
