  "number": "7.13",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The chamber quotients are the incidence varieties Each is isomorphic to . The projections are projective birational morphisms, are isomorphisms away from the origin, and have scheme-theoretic fiber over the origin. Their exceptional curves have codimension two and normal bundle .    On , normalizing leaves invariant coordinates , , and . Thus this quotient chart is , and its map to is The coordinates are listed in the order . On , use , , and . The overlap is These are the transition functions of two copies of the tautological line bundle on . They also describe the charts and of the stated incidence variety, proving the quotient identification scheme-theoretically.  For , the charts and have base coordinates and . Their fiber coordinates are and , with the same transition as in . This proves the assertions about and its incidence equations.  Both incidence varieties are closed in , so their projections to are projective and hence proper. A nonzero matrix of rank one has a unique column line and a unique row line. The lines depend regularly on the matrix on each open set where one of its entries is nonzero. Consequently each projection is an isomorphism away from the origin. Over the origin, the incidence equations impose no condition on the projective coordinate. In the affine charts, the fiber is defined by the two fiber coordinates, so it is the reduced zero section . Each total space is smooth of dimension three. The normal bundle of the zero section in the total space of a vector bundle is that vector bundle, giving the asserted normal bundle and codimension.   "
