  "number": "7.15",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The contractions are crepant. For the invariant divisors corresponding to , the degrees on their exceptional curves are The induced birational map over is the Atiyah flop. It is an isomorphism away from the exceptional curves and does not extend to a morphism over .    The character pairs to one with every ray. On all three varieties the canonical divisor is therefore the principal Cartier divisor . The contractions preserve the character and add no rays, so The Gorenstein assertion for is the criterion of . Thus this equality gives crepancy, including at its singular point.  The curve corresponds to the wall . Its adjacent rays satisfy Both adjacent cones are unimodular. The smooth wall calculation in the proof of consequently gives the first degree vector. That proof uses only the completeness of the curve and the two affine charts, so applies here without completeness of the threefold. For , the wall is , and the normalized relation is the negative of the displayed relation. This gives the second degree vector. Their coordinate sums are zero, also verifying .  Write for the vector bundle projections. The equation defines , so , which is relatively very ample for by the incidence embedding. On , the local equations of its strict transform are on and on . Since , the frames of have the transitions of . Hence is relatively very ample for . The two small contractions are crepant and reverse the relative sign of this Cartier divisor. This is the flop in the statement.  On the chart of , the projective coordinate of the induced point of is . Fix . Along it is , and along it is for . A morphism extending the birational map would give a morphism to on this chart. Its restrictions to these two affine lines would have the respective constant values also at , since is separated. The common origin cannot have both values. No such extension exists.   "
