  "type": "Proposition",
  "number": "11.3",
  "title": "Homogeneous families preserve the Hilbert function.",
  "body": " Homogeneous families preserve the Hilbert function  If is homogeneous in the standard grading, give degree one and degree zero. The algebra is graded, and each is a finite free -module of rank . Thus all fibers have the same graded Hilbert function. Moreover is a flat projective family with the corresponding projective fibers.  Write as the sum of its nonzero homogeneous parts . Then Its exponents in are nonnegative, so homogeneous elements suffice in the definition of . That ideal is therefore homogeneous in the indicated grading. Each is a quotient of the finite free module , hence finitely generated. It is torsion-free by the embedding in the theorem, and is therefore free over the PID. Its rank is computed after inverting , giving the stated number.  The relative Proj is closed in because is generated in degree one over . Its standard affine charts have rings . Localization preserves flatness, and the degree-zero part is a -module direct summand of the localized graded module. These rings are therefore flat. They cover the relative Proj, proving flatness of the projective family.  "
