  "number": "14.7",
  "title": "Positivity and its equality case.",
  "body": " Positivity and its equality case   The following conditions are equivalent: ; and for every continuous convex piecewise affine function on . Under these conditions, equality holds exactly for affine functions.    Assume . At each differentiability point of a convex function, its supporting-plane inequality at , evaluated at zero, gives Therefore . For completeness, the needed Jensen inequality follows from a supporting affine function at zero: write . Since the barycenter is zero, , and integration of proves the inequality.  If , both inequalities are equalities. Thus the continuous nonnegative function has integral zero on the full-dimensional polytope. It vanishes everywhere: any positive value, including at a boundary point, would by continuity give positive values on a subset of positive volume. Hence is affine. The affine formula proves the converse equality statement.  Conversely, affine functions and their negatives are convex piecewise affine functions. Nonnegativity on both forces their functional values to vanish, and gives .   "
