  "body": " A unique weighted balancing vector   There is a unique for which It is zero exactly when .    Consider . Differentiating under the integral gives the weighted barycenter as its gradient. Its Hessian in a nonzero direction is the variance of for the positive probability density proportional to . This variance is positive because has interior. Hence is strictly convex.  Choose a Euclidean ball . For , the ball of radius centered at , using the dual Euclidean identification, lies in and has . Integrating over it shows , with independent of . Thus attains a minimum, unique by strict convexity. Its gradient vanishes there. At zero its gradient is , proving the last assertion.   "
